WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.457
This is Lawrence Sperry

00:00:02.457 --> 00:00:05.631
aviation pioneer and&nbsp;certified daredevil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.631 --> 00:00:08.533
 He's about to fly his plane past a group of judges

00:00:08.533 --> 00:00:11.151
at the Paris Aviation&nbsp;Contest. 

00:00:11.151 --> 00:00:12.000
All of a sudden

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.000
his assistant climbs&nbsp;out onto the wing of the aircraft

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.383
causing it to&nbsp;suddenly veer to the right. 

00:00:17.383 --> 00:00:18.539
Instead of correcting&nbsp;it

00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:21.332
Lawrence takes his hands off the controls

00:00:21.332 --> 00:00:22.357
and miraculously, 

00:00:22.357 --> 00:00:25.892
the plane steers itself back on&nbsp;course.&nbsp;

00:00:25.892 --> 00:00:29.559
This is a real photo of that exact moment.&nbsp;

00:00:29.559 --> 00:00:30.945
On the next pass

00:00:30.945 --> 00:00:34.083
Lawrence himself climbs out onto the other wing &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.083 --> 00:00:38.488
and the plane continues to 
fly&nbsp;perfectly straight past the judges. 

00:00:38.488 --> 00:00:41.920
Sperry had&nbsp;invented the world's first autopilot. &nbsp;

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.431
Oh, and it&nbsp;won him the competition. 

00:00:44.431 --> 00:00:48.260
By taking a gyroscope and&nbsp;connecting it 
to the plane's rudder and elevators,

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:53.146
the gyroscope would command 
the controls to keep&nbsp;the plane completely level. 

00:00:53.146 --> 00:00:56.547
Using a gyroscope in&nbsp;this way 
was an engineering breakthrough

00:00:56.547 --> 00:00:58.353
And it&nbsp;went on to be used in everything 

00:00:58.353 --> 00:01:00.430
from cars, ships, rockets

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:02.730
all the way to today's phones. 

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:04.374
Over the&nbsp;course of 100 years

00:01:04.374 --> 00:01:09.670
all of this technology was&nbsp;condensed 
down into a tiny chip just 1 mmwide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:10.839
And because of this

00:01:10.839 --> 00:01:14.790
 your phone knows its position&nbsp;in space at all times. &nbsp;

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:16.619
But how does this work?&nbsp;

00:01:16.619 --> 00:01:18.720
The basic principle of a gyroscope 

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.677
is that if you&nbsp;spin a disc really quickly, 

00:01:21.677 --> 00:01:25.584
its angular momentum&nbsp;tries to keep it perfectly stable. 

00:01:25.584 --> 00:01:29.827
By placing&nbsp;the disc in a 
three-axis gimbal with very little&nbsp;friction, &nbsp;

00:01:29.827 --> 00:01:31.784
everything else will move around it, 

00:01:31.784 --> 00:01:34.615
but&nbsp;the disc will stay locked in place. 

00:01:34.615 --> 00:01:38.611
By measuring how much the 
gimbals have moved relative to the&nbsp;disc

00:01:38.611 --> 00:01:42.526
you can accurately measure 
how much the&nbsp;object has rotated. 

00:01:42.526 --> 00:01:46.689
Engineers in the 20's began 
to&nbsp;realize how useful this could be

00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:51.620
and gyroscopes&nbsp;like this started 
to appear in ships, rockets, and&nbsp;warplanes, 

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:54.699
just like the ones you can use in War&nbsp;Thunder.&nbsp;

