00:03:09.558 --> 00:03:11.487
In&nbsp;the midst of World War II, 

00:03:11.487 --> 00:03:15.641
Nazi Germany came out&nbsp;with this, the V2 rocket. 

00:03:15.641 --> 00:03:21.886
This was a long range&nbsp;missile designed 
to strike major cities from up&nbsp;to 200 m away. &nbsp;

00:03:21.886 --> 00:03:24.723
It was one of the first advanced&nbsp;rockets ever built, 

00:03:24.723 --> 00:03:27.000
and it was guided by a pair of&nbsp;gyroscopes &nbsp;

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.688
similar to those used on Spar's plane.&nbsp;

00:03:29.688 --> 00:03:33.259
During the flight, these would 
keep the rocket on a planned trajectory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.259 --> 00:03:35.497
and when the rocket reached&nbsp;a certain speed,

00:03:35.497 --> 00:03:40.532
it would cut off its engines 
and&nbsp;fall ballistically onto its target. 

00:03:40.532 --> 00:03:42.160
But in order&nbsp;for this to work

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.833
it had to know how fast it was&nbsp;going. 

00:03:44.833 --> 00:03:46.679
In another part of the guidance system,&nbsp;

00:03:46.679 --> 00:03:50.594
it used a very basic accelerometer 
to measure&nbsp;changes in speed. 

00:03:50.594 --> 00:03:54.880
This was essentially just a&nbsp;spinning disc 
with a weight added to one side.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.438
The disc could tilt around this axis 

00:03:57.438 --> 00:03:59.384
and the whole&nbsp;unit was on a platform 

00:03:59.384 --> 00:04:01.755
that could rotate around&nbsp;the vertical axis. 

00:04:01.755 --> 00:04:03.319
When the disc was up to speed

00:04:03.319 --> 00:04:06.480
it would keep it and the weight locked in place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.599
But when the rocket accelerated,

00:04:08.599 --> 00:04:13.489
the weight would&nbsp;want to stay where it is 
due to Newton's First&nbsp;Law of motion. 

00:04:13.489 --> 00:04:15.776
This would add a force to the disc&nbsp;

00:04:15.776 --> 00:04:17.758
causing it to tilt over. 

00:04:17.758 --> 00:04:21.628
As we know, if you try to&nbsp;tilt a fast spinning gyroscope,&nbsp;

00:04:21.628 --> 00:04:24.609
it will cause it&nbsp;to rotate around the vertical axis.

00:04:24.609 --> 00:04:26.506
This is called&nbsp;precession. &nbsp;

00:04:26.506 --> 00:04:29.881
The rocket's acceleration would cause&nbsp;the disc to tilt 

00:04:29.881 --> 00:04:32.916
and in turn the whole gyroscope&nbsp;would precess. &nbsp;

00:04:32.916 --> 00:04:34.898
The more the rocket accelerated

00:04:34.898 --> 00:04:37.277
the more the gyroscope would precess. 

00:04:37.277 --> 00:04:40.563
And so, by measuring exactly how much it precessed

00:04:40.563 --> 00:04:44.320
the&nbsp;onboard computer 
could measure its acceleration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:47.536
By accumulating all of the acceleration&nbsp;
measurements over time, 

00:04:47.536 --> 00:04:50.498
the current speed&nbsp;of the rocket could be calculated. 

00:04:50.498 --> 00:04:52.493
It was a genius&nbsp;system, 

00:04:52.493 --> 00:04:55.600
but in practice, it didn't work very well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:57.404
The V2 was inaccurate

00:04:57.404 --> 00:05:00.560
often missing entire cities&nbsp;by up to 10 m. 

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:04.575
Over time, the small amount 
of&nbsp;friction on each gimbal built up&nbsp;

00:05:04.575 --> 00:05:08.209
and gradually&nbsp;caused the gyroscope to drift off course. 

00:05:08.209 --> 00:05:11.093
This&nbsp;problem plagued gyroscopes for decades. &nbsp;

00:05:11.093 --> 00:05:13.726
But no&nbsp;matter how smoothly they were made, 

00:05:13.726 --> 00:05:16.779
there was&nbsp;no way to completely remove the friction. &nbsp;

00:05:16.779 --> 00:05:19.871
That&nbsp;was until they invented this.&nbsp;

00:05:19.871 --> 00:05:21.822
the ring laser gyroscope. &nbsp;

00:05:21.822 --> 00:05:24.289
With these, there were no moving parts.&nbsp;

00:05:24.289 --> 00:05:26.289
A laser would emit a beam of light 

00:05:26.289 --> 00:05:30.094
via a system of&nbsp;mirrors onto the same light sensor. &nbsp;

00:05:30.094 --> 00:05:31.649
Under normal&nbsp;circumstances

00:05:31.649 --> 00:05:34.792
the beams of light would 
travel the exact same distance &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.792 --> 00:05:37.820
and hit the sensor at the&nbsp;exact same time. 

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:40.637
Since both beams have the exact&nbsp;same frequency, 

00:05:40.637 --> 00:05:42.968
they would show up perfectly in&nbsp;sync. 

00:05:42.968 --> 00:05:45.167
But if the gyroscope rotates, 

00:05:45.167 --> 00:05:49.489
one beam of&nbsp;light will have to travel 
a slightly greater&nbsp;distance than the other one 

00:05:49.489 --> 00:05:52.242
and will therefore arrive slightly later. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.242 --> 00:05:56.400
The sensor would pick this&nbsp;up 
as a slight shift between the two frequencies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.362
The more the object rotated, 

00:05:58.362 --> 00:06:01.970
the more out of&nbsp;phase the two frequencies would be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:06.000
These ring laser gyroscopes 
were a major breakthrough in&nbsp;accuracy

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.116
and they could be made much smaller&nbsp;
than mechanical gyroscopes. &nbsp;

00:06:10.116 --> 00:06:11.234
Throughout the 80s

00:06:11.234 --> 00:06:15.000
they started being used in 
everything from rockets to airplanes, &nbsp;

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.861
replacing the old mechanical&nbsp;gyroscopes.

00:06:17.861 --> 00:06:19.920
But as technology developed&nbsp;

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:22.741
the need for an even smaller gyroscope grew, 

00:06:22.741 --> 00:06:25.030
and&nbsp;that's where we are now.

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:28.846
This tiny little thing is&nbsp;a MEMS device. &nbsp;

00:06:28.846 --> 00:06:31.549
It takes all of the concepts of the&nbsp;original gyro 

00:06:31.549 --> 00:06:35.572
and compresses them down 
onto a chip&nbsp;just 2 mm wide.&nbsp;

00:06:35.572 --> 00:06:38.746
Inside, it's made up of several&nbsp;smaller parts 

00:06:38.746 --> 00:06:41.473
three accelerometers and three gyroscopes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.473 --> 00:06:43.058
 one for each axis. 

00:06:43.058 --> 00:06:46.557
The intricate&nbsp;details that 
detect the movement are so small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.557 --> 00:06:49.125
that a bacteria could easily fit between them. 

00:06:49.125 --> 00:06:52.160
To&nbsp;see it working in action, you need a microscope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:55.265
but we don't have one, so here's an animation.&nbsp;

00:06:55.265 --> 00:06:57.920
First, let's look at how the accelerometers work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:01.123
Each one is made up of two main parts. 

00:07:01.123 --> 00:07:01.786
At the&nbsp;center

00:07:01.786 --> 00:07:03.760
there is a structure with sensing fingers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:06.282
that allows it to move back and forth. 

00:07:06.282 --> 00:07:09.000
This sits&nbsp;neatly inside a fixed structure 

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:12.242
that also has&nbsp;sensing fingers, but cannot move. &nbsp;

00:07:12.242 --> 00:07:15.587
Together, these&nbsp;two parts form a capacitance &nbsp;

00:07:15.587 --> 00:07:17.773
which is picked up by&nbsp;the sensing fingers. 

00:07:17.773 --> 00:07:20.663
When the object accelerates&nbsp;in this direction

00:07:20.663 --> 00:07:23.517
the central structure will want&nbsp;to stay where it is, &nbsp;

00:07:23.517 --> 00:07:26.147
just like the mass in the V2&nbsp;rocket. 

00:07:26.147 --> 00:07:27.935
If we zoom into the sensing fingers

00:07:27.935 --> 00:07:33.275
the&nbsp;capacitance here will go down 
and the capacitance&nbsp;here will go up. 

00:07:33.275 --> 00:07:35.275
The more the object accelerates,

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:38.047
the more it will affect the capacitance. 

00:07:38.047 --> 00:07:40.720
By&nbsp;taking the data from all three accelerometers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:42.607
and plotting it over time, 

00:07:42.607 --> 00:07:47.226
the phone's computer&nbsp;can 
figure out its movement in 3D space.

00:07:47.226 --> 00:07:50.688
 For the&nbsp;gyroscopes, things are a little bit trickier. &nbsp;

00:07:50.688 --> 00:07:53.200
A&nbsp;separate part within the central structure is made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:57.623
to constantly oscillate back 
and forth at an exact&nbsp;frequency. 

00:07:57.623 --> 00:08:00.766
Sensing fingers on the outside 
measure the capacitance, &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.766 --> 00:08:03.236
which should form a constant&nbsp;wave. 

00:08:03.236 --> 00:08:06.000
When the object rotates around this axis

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:10.534
it causes two parts at the center 
to tilt out&nbsp;in opposite directions. 

00:08:10.534 --> 00:08:14.129
This is much easier to understand 
if we look at a simple tuning&nbsp;fork&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.129 --> 00:08:16.960
 with both sides vibrating in sync.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:21.360
If we start to rotate the tuning fork&nbsp;
like this, thanks to the coriolis effect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:25.056
it pushes each fork out in opposite directions. 

00:08:25.056 --> 00:08:26.189
In&nbsp;the gyroscope, 

00:08:26.189 --> 00:08:31.269
the rotation of the object causes&nbsp;
the inner section to tilt in opposite directions.&nbsp;

00:08:31.269 --> 00:08:32.767
When these sections move

00:08:32.767 --> 00:08:36.108
the resonant frequency&nbsp;of 
the structure changes slightly.&nbsp;

00:08:36.108 --> 00:08:39.345
This slight&nbsp;change is picked up by the sensing fingers 

00:08:39.345 --> 00:08:42.000
as a&nbsp;slightly lower or higher frequency. 

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:44.774
When all axes&nbsp;are combined together

00:08:44.774 --> 00:08:49.218
the phone's computer can 
calculate its exact orientation in space. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.218 --> 00:08:55.623
Perhaps&nbsp;nothing shows the last century of technological&nbsp;evolution more than these gyroscopes. &nbsp;

00:08:55.623 --> 00:08:58.635
Thanks&nbsp;for watching 
and I'll see you in the next video.

